WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.780
How does a car actually rotate when reversing?

00:03.440 --> 00:06.540
Its rear wheels are fixed parallel to the sides of the car.

00:06.980 --> 00:10.920
That means the imaginary line passing through both rear wheel centers

00:10.920 --> 00:15.560
must always point directly at the center of the turning circle, labeled X.

00:16.200 --> 00:17.360
What about the front wheels?

00:17.960 --> 00:22.220
Because the inside front wheel travels along a tighter circle than the outside wheel,

00:22.560 --> 00:25.980
it has to steer at a sharper angle to avoid dragging sideways.

00:26.660 --> 00:28.360
This is called Ackerman Steering.

00:28.360 --> 00:31.880
Patented back in 1818 for horse-drawn carriages,

00:32.440 --> 00:38.720
when all wheel axle lines meet at a single point X, every part of the car turns cleanly around X.

